meta {
  name: Pair Complete
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/auth/pair/complete
  body: json
  auth: none
}

body:json {
  {
    "pairing_code": "{{pairing_code}}",
    "device_name": "{{device_name}}",
    "fcm_token": "{{fcm_token}}"
  }
}

script:post-response {
  const body = res.getBody();
  if (body && body.device_id && body.device_token) {
    bru.setEnvVar("device_id", body.device_id);
    bru.setEnvVar("device_token", body.device_token);
  }
}

docs {
  Exchange the 8-digit pairing code (read from the server terminal) for a
  long-lived device credential pair.

  Request body:
    pairing_code  — 8 digits, printed only in the server terminal
    device_name   — human-readable label for this device
    fcm_token     — optional. If present (and non-empty), it is registered
                    against the device atomically with pairing — equivalent
                    to calling PATCH /me/fcm-token after login but in one
                    round-trip. Empty string is treated as absent.

  Success response (200):
    { "device_id": "<uuid>", "device_token": "<64-hex>" }
    Both values are auto-saved into the Bruno env (device_id, device_token).
    STORE THE TOKEN — it is only shown once and is what /auth/login needs.

  Failures:
    410 PAIRING_EXPIRED  → no active code, code mismatched, or attempts cap hit
    429 (rate limit)

  Wrong-code attempts: each pairing window allows up to 3 wrong attempts
  before the state is wiped. Combined with the 8-digit space (~26 bits) and
  the 5-min TTL this puts online brute-force well out of reach.

  Code comparison is constant-time (subtle::ConstantTimeEq). On success the
  state is taken atomically — the code becomes one-shot.
}
